00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.657
It's a goody bag day.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:25.525
It's a good day.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:34.234
Related to the goody
bags, once again-- hello.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.770
Once again, I want
to get these out

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.105
for you, because you got to
have them on a Friday night.

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.608
There's no real
better way to think

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:44.010
about spending your
time on a Friday night

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.646
than opening up a
brand new goody bag.

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:51.051
The topic of the
goody bag is related

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.753
to reactions, which is the
topic we're starting today.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:58.224
But it's really also
related to acids and bases,

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:00.727
which is something we'll
talk about on Monday.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.362
So I'm giving you
the goody bag today,

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.897
but I'll talk
about the goody bag

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:07.934
and contextualize it in
the lecture on Monday.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.971
But as I said, today
what we're going to do

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.141
is we're going to start
a new topic that we'll

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.544
be talking about for the
next couple of weeks.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:20.013
And that is the
topic of reactions.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.015
And what we're going to
talk about is reactions, OK?

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:26.553
Now this falls under
the domain of something

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.289
called chemical
kinetics, which is

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.092
this study of reaction rates.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:34.761
Kinetic, move, right?

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.729
So what's moving?

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.465
Concentration, right?

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:42.902
This stuff that you started with
is doing something and winding

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.771
up different.

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.339
That's a reaction, OK?

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.008
Now we've already written lots
of reactions in this class,

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:53.513
but today, we're going
to talk about the rates.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.515
And the rate in particular
is the changing concentration

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:59.018
of the reactions and
products with time.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.121
So there's a couple
of terms that we

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:04.624
got to know because we're
going to be working with them.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.560
So I'm going to start
by writing those down.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.630
So the first thing is
the thing that we're

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:15.101
going to talk about changing,
which is the concentration.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:24.310
So the concentration is
equal to the moles per liter.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.679
I mean, there are lots of
ways to write concentration.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.548
This is the way we're
going to write it,

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:31.417
moles of a substance per liter.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.685
Well, that has a name for it.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.021
That's called them the molarity.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.089
What's molarity?

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.192
It's moles per liter.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.861
And then we can also
write it in shorthand,

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:47.734
which I'll be doing as simply
some substance in brackets.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:53.139
If I say that I have some
substance A, and now I say,

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:55.742
oh, look, I'm writing
brackets around it,

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:59.646
then what I mean is the
concentration of A, OK?

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:00.914
That's what that means.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:04.584
Which is the moles
per liter of A.

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:05.985
Good, so that's concentration.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:06.486
What else?

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:07.554
Well, there's the rate.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:08.988
There is the rate.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:13.660
And the rate is, as you
might guess, the change

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.363
in the concentration with time.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.799
That's the rate that we're
going to be talking about a lot

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.834
today, the reaction rate.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.304
There's a change in the
concentration with time.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.305
What else do we got?

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:28.141
Well, we have then a law.

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:35.781
Because see, here's the thing,
the rate is just the rate.

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:38.384
It's the change with
something with time, right?

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.987
It's like the change
in distance with time

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:42.388
would be like velocity.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.557
OK, but what if I
wanted to say, well,

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:49.896
could you tell me what the
rate is as some function?

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.598
Right, as some function?

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:53.266
That's called the rate law.

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.400
That's called the rate law.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.306
And it will give me a
rate versus concentration.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.176
That's what I want
out of a rate law.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:06.913
And then, well, I
could integrate that.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.515
I could integrate
that because the rate

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.618
itself is a
derivative with time.

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:12.552
It's a change in time.

00:04:12.552 --> 00:04:14.454
So if I integrate
it, well then I've

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.557
got something called
the integrated rate law.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:24.130
Integrated rate law.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.531
And what's that
going to give me?

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:29.836
It's going to give me the
concentration versus time.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:35.875
So the rate law is a rate
versus concentration.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:40.613
And the integrated rate law is
the concentration versus time.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:43.082
And then we've got Arrhenius.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.951
Let's put it all
on the same board.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:50.390
So I'll do one more here,
which is the Arrhenius

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:55.995
dependence for reactions.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:59.732
And as you might guess,
this is going to give me

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:05.972
rate versus temperature.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:08.474
These are the things we're
going to be talking about.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.410
These are all the things--
all of these things

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.646
we're going to talk about today
in the context of reactions.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.882
I wanted to get them
all down on the board

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:18.618
so you can see them
and think about them

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:22.555
and feel them as we go forward.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:25.457
So there are a whole
bunch of things

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:29.696
that impact the reaction,
the rate of a reaction.

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:30.730
What is it that matters?

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:31.864
Well, concentration.

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:33.332
How much do you have?

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.802
That's the brackets
thing, right?

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:40.840
Temperature,
catalysts, the surface

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.077
and the structure that
you're doing the reaction on,

00:05:44.077 --> 00:05:46.679
the solvent that it's in.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.982
All of these things impact
how the reaction happens.

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:52.452
As you can see, I grayed
out, made a little darker

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:55.088
those bottom two because I'm not
going to talk about those two.

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:58.391
But I will talk about each
of those other items today.

00:05:58.391 --> 00:05:59.459
That's our goal.

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:03.463
How do those items play
into these dependencies?

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:05.698
That's our goal,
to figure that out.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:09.235
OK, so we're going to
start with concentration.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.804
And we're going to talk
about how concentration

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.207
plays a role in reaction rates.

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:16.609
And we're going to start
with something very simple.

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:20.880
I have something called
A. Oh, it's purple.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.584
And it turns into something
called B. It's green.

00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:25.184
There you go.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:26.853
There's a reaction.

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.654
OK, so let's start--

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:33.926
OK, so aA goes to bB.

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:38.965
Now the very first thing that
I can think about in this

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:42.101
is that nothing is lost.

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:45.004
Nothing is lost.

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:47.407
So I'm going to
assume that nothing

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:49.509
disappeared from my container.

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:50.042
You see that?

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.811
The container is there.

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:54.213
So I haven't lost any mass.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:55.548
I haven't lost any mass.

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:59.252
Well, mass conservation
is a big deal, right?

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:03.556
So mass conservation-- by
taking this very simple case,

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:06.159
mass conser--

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:08.561
oh, really?

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:11.564
We could have a mass
conversation too.

00:07:11.564 --> 00:07:12.165
That's OK.

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:14.100
That's kind of what we're doing.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.436
But let's write it
as conservation.

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:20.907
This means that
if I take away A,

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:25.611
I must add B with the same
amount and not lose anything.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:28.948
And if you look at that in
terms of these coefficients,

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:33.052
remember, these are
stoichiometric coefficients

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:34.454
as part of the reaction.

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:39.392
Then what that means
is that 1 over b

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:45.665
times the change in the
concentration of B with time

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:50.303
must equal minus 1
over a times the change

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.473
in concentration of A with time.

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:56.008
That is mass
conservation, just written

00:07:56.008 --> 00:08:01.714
as changes in concentration.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:02.849
You can see it.

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:05.284
Look, imagine just as--

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:13.059
imagine that this is 1,
just as an example, OK?

00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:19.999
1H2 goes to 2H, right?

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.300
I'm not going to lose anything.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.503
Those are my
stoichio-- well, OK,

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:26.405
this would tell you
that the rate of this

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:29.175
if I don't lose
anything would be

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:36.182
1/2 the change in H, the
concentration of H with time

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:39.919
equals minus the change
in concentration of H2.

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:44.757
That's just exactly what I
wrote, but with an example.

00:08:44.757 --> 00:08:45.992
I didn't lose anything, right?

00:08:45.992 --> 00:08:48.694
So the rate of change of
this must be twice the rate

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:50.963
of change at that, right?

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:55.835
Stoichiometric coefficients
come in and they make sure--

00:08:55.835 --> 00:08:57.303
when I write it
this way, they make

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:59.572
sure I don't lose anything.

00:08:59.572 --> 00:09:01.507
That's why you've got
to have these there.

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:04.944
Now there's a couple other
things about this, all right?

00:09:04.944 --> 00:09:08.314
There's a couple other
things about this.

00:09:08.314 --> 00:09:16.222
One is that you'll notice that
the rate is always positive.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:18.391
Maybe we should write
that up on the top board.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.927
Because it's a convention.

00:09:20.927 --> 00:09:24.163
But we like positive numbers
when we talk about rate.

00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:29.702
So the rate is
always positive, OK?

00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:32.171
Always positive.

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:34.473
So when we have--

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:35.975
when we're thinking
about a reaction

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:39.211
where something's
getting consumed,

00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:41.414
we're going to write it as
the negative of the change

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:42.348
in that concentration.

00:09:42.348 --> 00:09:43.816
Because that's a
negative, and then

00:09:43.816 --> 00:09:46.352
we're going to write it as
a positive of the change

00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:48.754
in concentration of
things being formed, OK?

00:09:48.754 --> 00:09:52.558
So the rate is
written as positive.

00:09:52.558 --> 00:09:54.193
Well, there is
another thing, which

00:09:54.193 --> 00:09:56.262
is we can go more general.

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:57.430
Let's just think about this.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:01.968
If we go more
general, more general,

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:04.837
then let's have more
than just A to B.

00:10:04.837 --> 00:10:13.512
Let's have aA plus bB
goes to cC plus dD.

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:15.314
So now I've got four things.

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:18.417
Two products, two
reactants, right?

00:10:18.417 --> 00:10:21.087
OK A and B are the reactants,
C and D are the products.

00:10:21.087 --> 00:10:24.657
And they've all got
their coefficients.

00:10:24.657 --> 00:10:29.228
And so you can look at the
change in any one of these

00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:31.263
and know the rate.

00:10:31.263 --> 00:10:33.332
You could look at the
change in any one of these

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:34.333
and know the rate.

00:10:34.333 --> 00:10:39.905
So if I look at this, then the
rate would be equal to minus 1

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:41.674
over a, great convention.

00:10:41.674 --> 00:10:44.877
OK, it's a positive value.

00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:51.017
That's got to be equal to minus
1 over b times the change in B

00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:51.617
with time.

00:10:51.617 --> 00:10:57.289
And it must be equal to plus 1
over C, times the change in C

00:10:57.289 --> 00:10:58.057
with time.

00:10:58.057 --> 00:11:00.059
And that also must
be equal to plus 1

00:11:00.059 --> 00:11:05.931
over d, all right, times the
change in the concentration d

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:08.567
change dt.

00:11:08.567 --> 00:11:11.103
Right?

00:11:11.103 --> 00:11:14.273
So I could look at the way
any one of these things

00:11:14.273 --> 00:11:18.344
is disappearing or forming and
because those stoichiometric

00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:21.580
coefficients are there, I know
what the rate of that reaction

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:22.314
is.

00:11:22.314 --> 00:11:29.321
Again, this is basically a
statement of mass conservation.

00:11:29.321 --> 00:11:32.324
Ah, but there's more
because this is just

00:11:32.324 --> 00:11:33.993
a definition of rate.

00:11:33.993 --> 00:11:38.664
Rate is changing concentration
and it's always positive, done.

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:40.900
But I want a law.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.969
I want to know
not just OK, yeah,

00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:46.639
I see how you change with
time so that's a rate.

00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:49.208
But I want to know a
law, that if I gave you

00:11:49.208 --> 00:11:53.179
any concentration, if I gave
you any set of concentrations,

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:55.214
what would the rate be?

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:56.248
What would the rate be?

00:11:56.248 --> 00:11:58.350
Could I come up with a function?

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:04.623
And so we write down
the rate as depending

00:12:04.623 --> 00:12:07.126
on the concentrations.

00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:09.295
Now, you can do this with
the products or reactants.

00:12:09.295 --> 00:12:11.564
We're going to do this
with the reactants.

00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:14.133
And so you would
have that it depends

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:17.670
on the concentrations
of a and b raised

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.139
to some power with
some coefficient.

00:12:20.139 --> 00:12:24.977
So k times a concentration
of A to the m

00:12:24.977 --> 00:12:28.247
times the concentration
of B to the n.

00:12:28.247 --> 00:12:30.449
OK, so we're raising these--

00:12:30.449 --> 00:12:34.553
Now, we don't know what these
are, but what we're saying

00:12:34.553 --> 00:12:37.857
is that there's got to
be a way if I just know,

00:12:37.857 --> 00:12:40.259
and maybe if I do some
experiments or something,

00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:43.095
maybe I could come up with
a general function that

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:46.232
depends on just the
concentrations, wherever

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:47.833
they are of a and b.

00:12:47.833 --> 00:12:49.802
And they're going to be
raised to some exponent

00:12:49.802 --> 00:12:51.604
that I don't know
yet and they're

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:54.006
going to have some constant.

00:12:54.006 --> 00:12:55.441
That's a rate law.

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:56.442
That's a rate.

00:12:56.442 --> 00:12:57.943
OK?

00:12:57.943 --> 00:13:03.082
Now, OK, the thing is
that the rate law--

00:13:03.082 --> 00:13:05.451
let's go over to here.

00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:05.951
OK.

00:13:05.951 --> 00:13:09.922
So the k is, as you
can imagine going

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:13.159
to be very important here,
that's called a rate constant.

00:13:13.159 --> 00:13:14.827
K is equal to a rate constant.

00:13:20.466 --> 00:13:25.271
And this is going to depend on
conditions like temperature,

00:13:25.271 --> 00:13:26.939
pressure, solvent.

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:29.308
So it's going to depend on
things like temperature,

00:13:29.308 --> 00:13:33.279
pressure, solvent, et cetera.

00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:34.013
All right?

00:13:34.013 --> 00:13:38.117
And we'll see how you get k.

00:13:38.117 --> 00:13:41.787
But m and n cannot come--
and this is very important--

00:13:41.787 --> 00:13:46.792
m and n and k for that
matter must come--

00:13:46.792 --> 00:13:48.561
Gesundheit.

00:13:48.561 --> 00:13:52.198
--from experiments.

00:13:52.198 --> 00:13:54.667
They're determined
experimentally,

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:58.537
determined experiments.

00:13:58.537 --> 00:13:59.038
OK?

00:14:02.975 --> 00:14:06.011
This is a mistake
that is often made.

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:09.582
You see, m and n must somehow be
related to these coefficients.

00:14:09.582 --> 00:14:10.282
No.

00:14:10.282 --> 00:14:12.618
M and n is something else.

00:14:12.618 --> 00:14:15.754
I've created a function
that the rate depends on,

00:14:15.754 --> 00:14:17.890
the rate of this
reaction depends on.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.558
I've put these
exponents in there

00:14:19.558 --> 00:14:22.995
and I'm looking for the
dependents on m and n.

00:14:22.995 --> 00:14:24.964
And the only way to
do that, to know that,

00:14:24.964 --> 00:14:26.699
is to do experiments.

00:14:26.699 --> 00:14:29.468
You can't just get that from
the way the reaction is written.

00:14:29.468 --> 00:14:34.273
You can get mass conservation
and the relationship

00:14:34.273 --> 00:14:37.743
between rate, rate, rate.

00:14:37.743 --> 00:14:38.510
Right?

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:40.579
Those are all the same.

00:14:40.579 --> 00:14:43.883
But you can't get the
rate law unless you

00:14:43.883 --> 00:14:45.050
do some measurements.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:48.787
Now, then when you
do, then you can

00:14:48.787 --> 00:14:51.190
get the order of the reaction.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:55.961
So this is the reaction
order and that's

00:14:55.961 --> 00:15:03.269
an important property.

00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:04.970
There's one more thing
I'll say, and then

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:07.606
what we're going to do,
I'm setting the stage here

00:15:07.606 --> 00:15:09.174
and then we're going
to do examples.

00:15:09.174 --> 00:15:09.675
OK?

00:15:09.675 --> 00:15:12.578
So don't worry, we're going to
go through different examples

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:17.917
that I think will help
crystallize these concepts.

00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:20.519
But the last thing I want to say
in terms of setting the stage

00:15:20.519 --> 00:15:25.391
is that the units
of rate, if you

00:15:25.391 --> 00:15:27.192
look at how I've defined
the units of rate,

00:15:27.192 --> 00:15:30.996
it's a change in
concentration with time.

00:15:30.996 --> 00:15:39.638
So the units of rate,
the units of rate

00:15:39.638 --> 00:15:45.878
are going to be
molarity over time.

00:15:45.878 --> 00:15:48.914
That doesn't change.

00:15:48.914 --> 00:15:49.682
Right?

00:15:49.682 --> 00:15:53.419
That doesn't change, those
are the units of rate.

00:15:53.419 --> 00:15:55.654
That's the definition
of the rate.

00:15:55.654 --> 00:15:58.424
But you can see,
and we will see,

00:15:58.424 --> 00:16:02.494
that if that has to always
be true, then the units of k

00:16:02.494 --> 00:16:04.363
may very.

00:16:04.363 --> 00:16:08.000
And we're going to see that
as we go through examples.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.237
So the units of k could
be different and we'll be

00:16:12.237 --> 00:16:15.774
depending on the reaction
order, which is basically saying

00:16:15.774 --> 00:16:18.777
it depends on the rate law.

00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:21.347
OK, setting the stage.

00:16:21.347 --> 00:16:23.415
Now, let's look
at some examples.

00:16:23.415 --> 00:16:25.317
And we're going to go
through this sequential,

00:16:25.317 --> 00:16:27.219
we're going to do a
zeroth order reaction,

00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:30.389
than a first order reaction, and
then a second order reaction.

00:16:30.389 --> 00:16:30.990
OK?

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.024
So zeroth order.

00:16:32.024 --> 00:16:36.028
Well, zeroth order
reaction, right?

00:16:36.028 --> 00:16:37.763
So this is nitrous oxide.

00:16:37.763 --> 00:16:43.602
So this is a reaction of nitrous
oxide turning into N2 and O2.

00:16:43.602 --> 00:16:48.307
Nitrous oxide is used in
many, many applications,

00:16:48.307 --> 00:16:49.641
not just laughing gas.

00:16:53.912 --> 00:16:57.649
If we go with what
we just wrote down.

00:16:57.649 --> 00:16:59.284
OK, so let's take
this example, I'm

00:16:59.284 --> 00:17:00.719
going to write it down here.

00:17:00.719 --> 00:17:08.260
2 N20 goes to 2 N2 plus O2.

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:09.428
OK.

00:17:09.428 --> 00:17:11.864
Well, so the rate we know
from what I wrote down,

00:17:11.864 --> 00:17:17.036
the rate is equal to
minus 1/2 times the change

00:17:17.036 --> 00:17:20.138
in the concentration
of N20 with time.

00:17:24.309 --> 00:17:26.979
But see, so now I'm
doing some measurements

00:17:26.979 --> 00:17:30.516
and I'm plotting some data here.

00:17:30.516 --> 00:17:36.188
So I'm plotting here the
concentration of all three

00:17:36.188 --> 00:17:40.392
of these, the reaction, and
the two products with time.

00:17:40.392 --> 00:17:43.595
And what you notice is if I plot
the concentration with time,

00:17:43.595 --> 00:17:46.165
it's a linear relationship.

00:17:46.165 --> 00:17:48.967
What does that mean?

00:17:48.967 --> 00:17:53.038
That means that they don't
depend on concentration

00:17:53.038 --> 00:17:56.942
because the rate is
the change right,

00:17:56.942 --> 00:17:59.945
the rate is the change in
those with time, right?

00:17:59.945 --> 00:18:02.114
The change in the
concentration of time

00:18:02.114 --> 00:18:05.350
is always the same because
it's a straight line.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:12.825
So if that's true,
then the rate from,

00:18:12.825 --> 00:18:16.562
let's say, straight
line, straight line,

00:18:16.562 --> 00:18:18.730
and we'll talk about
plots as we go,

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:26.738
straight line plot of
concentration versus time.

00:18:26.738 --> 00:18:28.941
All right, straight
line plot, then we

00:18:28.941 --> 00:18:42.654
know that concentration
is independent of time.

00:18:42.654 --> 00:18:43.155
I'm sorry.

00:18:45.724 --> 00:18:49.061
Rate is independent.

00:18:49.061 --> 00:18:50.095
OK, hold on.

00:18:50.095 --> 00:18:54.566
Rate is independent
of concentration.

00:18:54.566 --> 00:18:55.868
OK.

00:18:55.868 --> 00:18:59.004
The rate in the beginning is
the change in concentration.

00:18:59.004 --> 00:19:00.539
The rate in the
middle is the change

00:19:00.539 --> 00:19:02.808
in concentration with time
and it's a straight line

00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:05.611
so it's always the same.

00:19:05.611 --> 00:19:06.979
Well, that means something.

00:19:06.979 --> 00:19:09.882
It means something in
terms of our rate law.

00:19:09.882 --> 00:19:13.418
Because now I know
from my rate law,

00:19:13.418 --> 00:19:17.890
that the rate is equal
to k, the rate constant,

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:22.761
times N20 to the m.

00:19:22.761 --> 00:19:24.396
That's from here, right?

00:19:24.396 --> 00:19:26.765
You take all the reactants
and you put them down

00:19:26.765 --> 00:19:28.700
and you put exponents on them.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:33.639
And so I've just got one,
N20 and it's raised to the m.

00:19:33.639 --> 00:19:37.342
Yeah, but I know there's
no concentration dependents

00:19:37.342 --> 00:19:38.010
of this rate.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:42.014
So I know that m
equals zero and it's

00:19:42.014 --> 00:19:47.553
a zeroth order reaction, rxn.

00:19:47.553 --> 00:19:51.723
Oh, I've saved so much
time, rxn reaction,

00:19:51.723 --> 00:19:55.460
which means that the rate
is simply equal to k.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.963
The rate must equal k, OK?

00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:05.704
So in this case the rate,
right, so we're continuing here

00:20:05.704 --> 00:20:07.472
equals k.

00:20:07.472 --> 00:20:21.186
And that means that k must have
units of molarity over time.

00:20:21.186 --> 00:20:24.690
Why, because that's the units
of rate, the units of rate

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.859
don't change, the
units of k will.

00:20:27.859 --> 00:20:31.530
But in a zeroth order
reaction, the units of k

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:35.067
have to be equal to
the units of rate

00:20:35.067 --> 00:20:37.336
that there's nothing
else in there.

00:20:37.336 --> 00:20:38.303
OK.

00:20:38.303 --> 00:20:39.805
Again, I want to
emphasize, there

00:20:39.805 --> 00:20:43.375
is no simple correlation
between the stoichiometry,

00:20:43.375 --> 00:20:47.479
the coefficients of these
things and the rate law,

00:20:47.479 --> 00:20:48.814
you've got to do experiments.

00:20:48.814 --> 00:20:50.215
This data comes from--

00:20:50.215 --> 00:20:53.885
Well look at it, there's the
mass conservation in action,

00:20:53.885 --> 00:20:56.622
02, N2, all right?

00:20:56.622 --> 00:20:57.923
You can see it.

00:20:57.923 --> 00:21:00.058
You can feel it.

00:21:00.058 --> 00:21:04.730
Slopes are different,
they must be.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:07.899
Now, here's a reaction
some of you may care about.

00:21:07.899 --> 00:21:11.270
So why does a zeroth
order reaction matter.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:15.040
Why does it matter, because
that's the reaction of beer.

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:20.579
So ethanol, OK, but
we'll call it beer.

00:21:20.579 --> 00:21:23.215
So what does this mean?

00:21:23.215 --> 00:21:28.520
If I consume beer, if I
did, then the concentration

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:33.392
of ethanol in it, there it is
plotted versus time is linear.

00:21:33.392 --> 00:21:35.727
That means, this
is very important,

00:21:35.727 --> 00:21:38.163
it's a zeroth order reaction.

00:21:38.163 --> 00:21:40.932
It means if I plot the rate
of the reaction, you see it?

00:21:40.932 --> 00:21:41.433
There it is.

00:21:41.433 --> 00:21:46.438
It's a constant, it doesn't
depend on the concentration.

00:21:46.438 --> 00:21:49.241
But that really has a
lot of ramifications,

00:21:49.241 --> 00:21:52.411
because if I drink a lot of
beer, the average 70 kilogram

00:21:52.411 --> 00:21:57.482
person, it takes 2 and 1/2 hours
for the enzymes in their liver

00:21:57.482 --> 00:22:02.587
to decompose 15 milliliters
of ethanol, one beer.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.792
But look at that, so
that means that if I

00:22:06.792 --> 00:22:11.830
had more than that
the body, it's not

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:14.099
dependent on how much I have.

00:22:14.099 --> 00:22:18.403
The reaction of the
liver, the enzymes

00:22:18.403 --> 00:22:19.905
that are breaking
this down is not

00:22:19.905 --> 00:22:21.206
dependent on the concentration.

00:22:21.206 --> 00:22:24.109
So if I load up a lot more
in there it doesn't matter.

00:22:24.109 --> 00:22:25.877
There is a pipeline
and there's a rate

00:22:25.877 --> 00:22:32.284
and it's not changing, well
that leads to consequences

00:22:32.284 --> 00:22:35.387
to be thought about
when you drink beer.

00:22:35.387 --> 00:22:37.322
But there's another thing
we can do with beer,

00:22:37.322 --> 00:22:39.291
which is we can integrate it.

00:22:39.291 --> 00:22:40.759
Right?

00:22:40.759 --> 00:22:47.366
Because if we know that beer--

00:22:47.366 --> 00:22:48.333
if we know that minus--

00:22:48.333 --> 00:22:50.535
I'm going to say there's
some reaction that beer goes

00:22:50.535 --> 00:22:51.169
to something.

00:22:51.169 --> 00:22:59.411
So minus d beer, dt OK?

00:22:59.411 --> 00:23:01.513
Assuming it has a
coefficient of 1,

00:23:01.513 --> 00:23:08.153
is going to equal, that's
going to equal k because it's

00:23:08.153 --> 00:23:09.988
a zeroth order reaction.

00:23:09.988 --> 00:23:11.556
Right, it's a constant.

00:23:11.556 --> 00:23:14.926
And so OK, now I
integrate both sides.

00:23:14.926 --> 00:23:19.598
If I integrate, then I've
got the integrated right law

00:23:19.598 --> 00:23:23.935
for beer, which is that
the concentration of beer

00:23:23.935 --> 00:23:31.009
is equal to some initial
concentration of beer minus kt.

00:23:31.009 --> 00:23:33.211
This is the integrated rate law.

00:23:33.211 --> 00:23:34.312
That's the integrated law.

00:23:34.312 --> 00:23:36.715
Notice what I've done,
I've gone from talking

00:23:36.715 --> 00:23:39.851
about the rate of the
beer being the rate there,

00:23:39.851 --> 00:23:44.055
which is a constant
to a dependence.

00:23:44.055 --> 00:23:45.490
There it is,
integrated rate law.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:48.927
The promise was that would get
me a concentration versus time

00:23:48.927 --> 00:23:52.330
and that's what it did,
concentration versus time.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:53.131
How did I get it?

00:23:53.131 --> 00:23:57.235
Well, I had a rate law
and I integrated it.

00:23:57.235 --> 00:23:58.703
And I'm not going
through the math,

00:23:58.703 --> 00:24:02.240
but you put the dt over
here and you integrate, OK?

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:03.074
OK.

00:24:03.074 --> 00:24:05.277
So that's an
integrated rate law.

00:24:05.277 --> 00:24:07.245
Well, let's go to
the next order.

00:24:07.245 --> 00:24:11.450
If I had first order and I
looked at the data for that,

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:17.589
then you would see if I plot
the concentration versus time

00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:19.658
it would not be a straight line.

00:24:19.658 --> 00:24:20.692
Why?

00:24:20.692 --> 00:24:22.994
Well, because for a
first order reaction,

00:24:22.994 --> 00:24:24.229
so now we're in first order.

00:24:28.733 --> 00:24:35.674
Right, then what that means is
that the exponent here is a 1

00:24:35.674 --> 00:24:39.478
or if I have two reactants,
the addition of them,

00:24:39.478 --> 00:24:46.151
it's first order
total or it might

00:24:46.151 --> 00:24:48.987
be dependent on two
reactants and its first order

00:24:48.987 --> 00:24:51.556
in each of them, but then second
order overall because they're

00:24:51.556 --> 00:24:52.791
multiplied.

00:24:52.791 --> 00:24:56.728
Let's stay we the simpler
case of just one reactant A.

00:24:56.728 --> 00:25:00.432
So if there's one
reactant, and let's suppose

00:25:00.432 --> 00:25:04.002
A goes to some product.

00:25:04.002 --> 00:25:07.739
I'm not even going
to write it out.

00:25:07.739 --> 00:25:12.544
You know that the
rate is equal to minus

00:25:12.544 --> 00:25:18.750
the change in the
concentration of A with time

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.453
and that because
it's first order

00:25:21.453 --> 00:25:27.058
it depends on the concentration
raised to the first power.

00:25:27.058 --> 00:25:30.028
That's what we said
first order meant, right.

00:25:30.028 --> 00:25:32.597
I'm telling you this
is first order now.

00:25:32.597 --> 00:25:33.098
OK.

00:25:35.734 --> 00:25:38.036
Well, you can see that
if it's first order

00:25:38.036 --> 00:25:40.171
it depends on the concentration.

00:25:40.171 --> 00:25:42.240
And so that means that
you can see from that

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:43.475
and you can see from the plot.

00:25:43.475 --> 00:25:48.847
If the concentration of A
doubles the rate doubles.

00:25:48.847 --> 00:25:49.614
The rate doubles.

00:25:49.614 --> 00:25:53.818
Why can't our livers do that.

00:25:53.818 --> 00:25:55.887
No, they're zeroth
order, but there

00:25:55.887 --> 00:25:57.689
are lots of reactions
that are first order.

00:26:00.959 --> 00:26:04.095
OK, here's the thing
right, but the rate still

00:26:04.095 --> 00:26:08.800
has to have units of molarity
change with time, right

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:11.269
molarity over time.

00:26:11.269 --> 00:26:16.808
They can't have that
unless k units are k units,

00:26:16.808 --> 00:26:22.981
in this case, are going to be--

00:26:22.981 --> 00:26:25.250
So let's see, let
me write down--

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:30.388
OK rate has units.

00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:35.994
The overall units are
molarity over time.

00:26:35.994 --> 00:26:41.299
So now I've got
k times molarity,

00:26:41.299 --> 00:26:45.103
that has to have units
of molarity over time

00:26:45.103 --> 00:26:48.807
and so k must have
units of 1 over time.

00:26:48.807 --> 00:26:56.247
1 over time, but that
tells us what the plot.

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:58.750
It helps tell us
things about like--

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:01.886
But the plots always come
better when we integrate.

00:27:01.886 --> 00:27:05.757
If we think about the
integrated rate law,

00:27:05.757 --> 00:27:11.763
then it leads to linearity,
which is something

00:27:11.763 --> 00:27:13.298
we always want in plots.

00:27:13.298 --> 00:27:14.332
Linear lines.

00:27:14.332 --> 00:27:15.400
Straight lines.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:18.036
Linear lines, really,
I just said that?

00:27:18.036 --> 00:27:19.471
I did just say that.

00:27:19.471 --> 00:27:23.708
I'm OK with it
because it's true.

00:27:23.708 --> 00:27:26.878
But let's integrate this.

00:27:26.878 --> 00:27:30.915
Integrated rate law.

00:27:30.915 --> 00:27:33.051
So now, there's my right law.

00:27:33.051 --> 00:27:35.587
Now I'm going to take the dt
over and the concentration over

00:27:35.587 --> 00:27:38.456
and I'm going to
integrate and I get this,

00:27:38.456 --> 00:27:44.062
A equals some initial
concentration times e

00:27:44.062 --> 00:27:47.165
to the minus kt.

00:27:47.165 --> 00:27:49.000
Notice the units,
right 1 over time.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:50.301
That's good.

00:27:50.301 --> 00:27:52.404
OK, or if you
want, you could say

00:27:52.404 --> 00:28:00.211
that that's a Ln A
equals Ln a 0 minus kt

00:28:00.211 --> 00:28:03.081
and this right now,
this gave it to me.

00:28:03.081 --> 00:28:08.653
Because now you know that
if you plot Ln versus time,

00:28:08.653 --> 00:28:16.461
if I have a plot of Ln versus
time, plot of Ln versus time,

00:28:16.461 --> 00:28:17.495
then it's linear.

00:28:17.495 --> 00:28:22.567
And we like linear and that's
what's plotted on the right.

00:28:22.567 --> 00:28:24.703
So that's a plot
of Ln versus time.

00:28:24.703 --> 00:28:27.005
And so you can kind of
go backwards and forth.

00:28:27.005 --> 00:28:33.078
Like if I had a linear plot
with concentration versus time

00:28:33.078 --> 00:28:35.013
you know it's zeroth order.

00:28:35.013 --> 00:28:38.349
If I had a linear plot with
Ln concentration versus time

00:28:38.349 --> 00:28:40.351
you know it's first order.

00:28:40.351 --> 00:28:42.787
And you know that from
the integrated rate law.

00:28:46.057 --> 00:28:54.666
Now, if you have data, how
do I know what the rate law--

00:28:54.666 --> 00:28:57.802
I'm sorry, what the
reaction order is.

00:28:57.802 --> 00:29:00.205
Often you'll just have
data, you study a reaction

00:29:00.205 --> 00:29:01.072
and you get data.

00:29:01.072 --> 00:29:03.341
Here's a very
important reaction.

00:29:03.341 --> 00:29:06.244
This molecule is
called cisplatin.

00:29:06.244 --> 00:29:10.348
Now, cisplatin is
maybe the most used

00:29:10.348 --> 00:29:13.485
and one of the certainly, very
most important chemotherapy

00:29:13.485 --> 00:29:15.253
drugs.

00:29:15.253 --> 00:29:17.856
But it's not active
in this state

00:29:17.856 --> 00:29:19.124
with the two chlorine's there.

00:29:19.124 --> 00:29:24.028
You've got to get one of them
out and put a water molecule

00:29:24.028 --> 00:29:27.766
there, so that with
the water molecule

00:29:27.766 --> 00:29:30.602
it can go and damage
the DNA of the tumor.

00:29:30.602 --> 00:29:34.939
That's the idea and it can't
do that unless this reaction

00:29:34.939 --> 00:29:36.508
happens in the body.

00:29:36.508 --> 00:29:39.177
And so you can imagine
that this kind of reaction

00:29:39.177 --> 00:29:40.478
is extremely important.

00:29:40.478 --> 00:29:41.379
What?

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:45.650
And the rate is extremely
important here, all right.

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:49.087
This has to happen in a time
frame that we know very well.

00:29:49.087 --> 00:29:51.356
So this rate has been
studied very carefully

00:29:51.356 --> 00:29:52.123
for this reaction.

00:29:52.123 --> 00:29:53.892
And you can see, OK,
you can write down

00:29:53.892 --> 00:29:56.327
numbers, how much
did I start with

00:29:56.327 --> 00:29:59.831
and how much what
was the rate, and I'm

00:29:59.831 --> 00:30:03.201
watching the rate change.

00:30:03.201 --> 00:30:05.904
Right away you know
the rate changes

00:30:05.904 --> 00:30:12.210
as the cisplatin concentration
changes, it's not zeroth order.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:14.279
And the second
thing you know is,

00:30:14.279 --> 00:30:19.117
if you take any two of
those and you take ratios,

00:30:19.117 --> 00:30:23.087
then the ratios are the same
so it must be first order.

00:30:23.087 --> 00:30:26.357
That's one way to know
what order you have.

00:30:26.357 --> 00:30:27.992
All right.

00:30:27.992 --> 00:30:28.493
Yeah.

00:30:28.493 --> 00:30:30.328
So if I have data.

00:30:30.328 --> 00:30:36.601
With data like that, with
data you could do this.

00:30:36.601 --> 00:30:44.642
You could take ratios
at two different times.

00:30:48.913 --> 00:30:53.351
And what does that get,
it gets you the order.

00:30:53.351 --> 00:30:56.287
From that you can get the order
and we'll see another example.

00:30:56.287 --> 00:30:57.789
And then, you can
go further than

00:30:57.789 --> 00:31:01.960
that because then I could plug
in data from one of the times,

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:04.295
right.

00:31:04.295 --> 00:31:08.299
Once I know the order I
could use data from one time,

00:31:08.299 --> 00:31:14.539
from one time to get k.

00:31:14.539 --> 00:31:16.841
Because once I know
the order, I know

00:31:16.841 --> 00:31:19.611
how to write down the
rate law, and then I

00:31:19.611 --> 00:31:21.613
can use any of these
lines to get k.

00:31:24.949 --> 00:31:28.786
So we want to be
comfortable with reactions,

00:31:28.786 --> 00:31:33.591
with rates going back and
forth between order, rate law,

00:31:33.591 --> 00:31:34.826
integrated rate law, plots.

00:31:39.998 --> 00:31:45.169
So if I had data that looked
like this I could do the same.

00:31:45.169 --> 00:31:48.806
Here's another reaction, I
could say what order is it?

00:31:48.806 --> 00:31:50.508
I'm not going to do
it, I'll just say it.

00:31:50.508 --> 00:31:53.378
So you take any two
times and concentrations

00:31:53.378 --> 00:31:55.580
and what you have
is that the ratio

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:00.451
squared gives you the ratio of
the rates, that means it must

00:32:00.451 --> 00:32:01.753
be a second order reaction.

00:32:01.753 --> 00:32:04.188
It means it must be, right.

00:32:04.188 --> 00:32:08.059
I had something that
went into products

00:32:08.059 --> 00:32:11.396
and the dependence of
the rate from this data,

00:32:11.396 --> 00:32:15.099
the dependence of the rate is
that something's concentration

00:32:15.099 --> 00:32:17.101
squared and you can
see it from the data.

00:32:19.771 --> 00:32:24.709
So well, let's see.

00:32:24.709 --> 00:32:29.180
I'm going back to
the middle here.

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:36.621
OK, so now, we just
went to second order.

00:32:40.758 --> 00:32:47.999
So for second order
what do you have?

00:32:47.999 --> 00:32:55.340
Well, you have that minus d
of something, some reactant,

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.775
and I just got
one reactant here,

00:32:57.775 --> 00:33:00.478
if you think about it as
A. So the same thing A

00:33:00.478 --> 00:33:03.681
went to something, OK, so minus
the change in concentration

00:33:03.681 --> 00:33:08.319
of that with time is dependent
on k times the concentration

00:33:08.319 --> 00:33:09.921
of that squared.

00:33:09.921 --> 00:33:16.194
And if we integrate this one,
integrate then what do you get,

00:33:16.194 --> 00:33:19.564
you get 1 over the
concentration of the something

00:33:19.564 --> 00:33:24.869
equals 1 over its
initial value plus kt.

00:33:28.339 --> 00:33:29.040
You can see that.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:31.509
Take the t over here
and take this over there

00:33:31.509 --> 00:33:35.246
and then that's going to
be the integrated rate law.

00:33:35.246 --> 00:33:38.016
So now, look at that.

00:33:38.016 --> 00:33:40.218
What do I plot to get linear?

00:33:40.218 --> 00:33:44.255
I got get to linear, I don't
like curves in reaction rate

00:33:44.255 --> 00:33:48.226
plots, I like linear lines
because the linear line

00:33:48.226 --> 00:33:53.765
will tell me something
about the reaction order.

00:33:53.765 --> 00:33:56.200
And by the way, the slope
could tell me something

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:57.602
about the rate constant.

00:33:57.602 --> 00:33:58.736
Right?

00:33:58.736 --> 00:34:01.439
And so if it's second
order, the plot

00:34:01.439 --> 00:34:02.907
that gives you a
linear line is 1

00:34:02.907 --> 00:34:05.610
over concentration versus time.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.210
So let's look.

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:09.112
So let's say I had this
data and I try to plot it.

00:34:09.112 --> 00:34:12.183
Well, the first thing I do
is I say, well OK hold on,

00:34:12.183 --> 00:34:16.154
if I plot the concentration
versus time that's

00:34:16.154 --> 00:34:17.522
what it looks like, not linear.

00:34:20.558 --> 00:34:22.994
That means it's
not zeroth order.

00:34:22.994 --> 00:34:24.462
OK, I'm going to
take the same data

00:34:24.462 --> 00:34:28.800
and I'm going to plot the Ln
of it versus time, not linear.

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.437
Not first order, but if I
plot 1 over that concentration

00:34:32.437 --> 00:34:35.639
versus time it gives me
a perfect linear fit,

00:34:35.639 --> 00:34:37.942
so it must be second order.

00:34:37.942 --> 00:34:41.045
Plots, order, linear.

00:34:41.045 --> 00:34:43.648
OK?

00:34:43.648 --> 00:34:46.551
We're not going to go
beyond zeroth, first,

00:34:46.551 --> 00:34:48.052
and second order
because what I want

00:34:48.052 --> 00:34:51.755
is for you to feel your
oneness with reaction rates

00:34:51.755 --> 00:34:56.094
and kinetics through these
three orders, zeroth, 1, and 2,

00:34:56.094 --> 00:35:00.765
and through these different
concepts that are up there.

00:35:00.765 --> 00:35:07.405
So one more thing you can do,
before I turn to temperature,

00:35:07.405 --> 00:35:11.109
one more thing you can do once
you know the rate law is you

00:35:11.109 --> 00:35:15.379
can calculate how long it
takes for the concentration

00:35:15.379 --> 00:35:18.783
to be cut in half.

00:35:18.783 --> 00:35:24.922
If I know the rate law,
then I can calculate

00:35:24.922 --> 00:35:27.258
the half life, that's called.

00:35:27.258 --> 00:35:37.902
All right, so for example,
if it's first order

00:35:37.902 --> 00:35:44.308
let's suppose it's first order
then the integrated rate law--

00:35:44.308 --> 00:35:46.911
I'm just going to put the two
concentrations on the same side

00:35:46.911 --> 00:35:47.945
here--

00:35:47.945 --> 00:35:50.081
A over A0.

00:35:50.081 --> 00:35:51.482
OK.

00:35:51.482 --> 00:35:54.385
So the integrated rate
law gives me this.

00:35:54.385 --> 00:35:57.622
It's just what I had
before somewhere.

00:35:57.622 --> 00:36:02.727
OK, but you can see, well
what if the concentration of A

00:36:02.727 --> 00:36:05.062
is exactly 1/2 of the
beginning concentration.

00:36:05.062 --> 00:36:13.337
So when A equals 1/2 the
initial concentration,

00:36:13.337 --> 00:36:16.707
but then this becomes a 1/2.

00:36:16.707 --> 00:36:17.575
Right?

00:36:17.575 --> 00:36:22.914
So if I want to know how much
time it takes to get to 1/2,

00:36:22.914 --> 00:36:25.049
that's a good thing to
know just in general.

00:36:25.049 --> 00:36:28.953
How long do I have until
half of this is left?

00:36:28.953 --> 00:36:32.757
Well, then you know that this
is going to give you ln2.

00:36:32.757 --> 00:36:40.498
When A equals 1/2 A0
then ln2 equals kt.

00:36:40.498 --> 00:36:43.434
And we're going to
call that an important.

00:36:43.434 --> 00:36:46.671
It's not just any time,
it's called the half life,

00:36:46.671 --> 00:36:48.039
it's the time that
it took to get

00:36:48.039 --> 00:36:52.510
to A being 1/2 of the initial.

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:54.145
So I've got it.

00:36:54.145 --> 00:36:55.646
I've got it, I've
got the half life.

00:36:55.646 --> 00:36:58.482
The half life is a
very powerful tool.

00:36:58.482 --> 00:37:00.484
A number of you may have
heard of carbon dating.

00:37:00.484 --> 00:37:04.055
This is just a picture
of wild animals,

00:37:04.055 --> 00:37:05.957
but what happens,
those wild animals

00:37:05.957 --> 00:37:10.394
breath in oxygen and other stuff
from the air including stuff

00:37:10.394 --> 00:37:13.064
that contains a
spread of carbon atoms

00:37:13.064 --> 00:37:16.334
and we know carbon has isotopes.

00:37:16.334 --> 00:37:17.902
We know about carbons
isotopes, we've

00:37:17.902 --> 00:37:21.505
already talked about
them, c12, c13, c14.

00:37:21.505 --> 00:37:25.376
C14 has this wonderful
thing about it

00:37:25.376 --> 00:37:26.777
that it radioactivity decays.

00:37:29.447 --> 00:37:32.483
And it turns out that there's
a certain concentration of that

00:37:32.483 --> 00:37:33.951
in the atmosphere
at any given time

00:37:33.951 --> 00:37:37.488
and we can go back in history
and know how much concentration

00:37:37.488 --> 00:37:39.757
there was in the atmosphere.

00:37:39.757 --> 00:37:43.961
And we know if these
animals are breathing it in.

00:37:43.961 --> 00:37:49.634
We know that they should have
that concentration at A0,

00:37:49.634 --> 00:37:51.902
except this isn't a
concentration this

00:37:51.902 --> 00:37:53.104
is a number of atoms.

00:37:53.104 --> 00:37:56.474
But It's OK, it's still
a first order process.

00:37:56.474 --> 00:38:00.711
The radioactive decay of c14
is a first order process.

00:38:00.711 --> 00:38:04.715
And so, because of
that we can date,

00:38:04.715 --> 00:38:07.985
we can date things using
carbon dating very accurately.

00:38:07.985 --> 00:38:12.323
This is a really awesome tool
that relies simply on the half

00:38:12.323 --> 00:38:15.960
life, on knowing the half life.

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:21.098
All right, here's a summary
that I thought would be useful.

00:38:21.098 --> 00:38:21.699
This is it.

00:38:21.699 --> 00:38:23.734
This is everything we just
talked about before we

00:38:23.734 --> 00:38:24.869
switch to temperature.

00:38:24.869 --> 00:38:27.471
Rate law, zeroth
order, first order.

00:38:27.471 --> 00:38:29.740
And you're all like, why
did I write this all down,

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:30.741
it's all on this page.

00:38:30.741 --> 00:38:31.776
No, it's good to--

00:38:31.776 --> 00:38:34.445
Did somebody say, yeah.

00:38:34.445 --> 00:38:36.881
It's good to write down, it
helps you think about things.

00:38:36.881 --> 00:38:38.316
So here it all is.

00:38:38.316 --> 00:38:42.620
There is the zeroth order,
first order, second order, OK?

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:45.956
Integrated rate law
that comes from that.

00:38:45.956 --> 00:38:48.459
The units of the rate constant.

00:38:48.459 --> 00:38:52.296
The linear plot to
determine the rate constant.

00:38:52.296 --> 00:38:55.132
What gives you what the
slope of that line is

00:38:55.132 --> 00:38:57.401
and then what the half life
would be if you just follow

00:38:57.401 --> 00:38:59.170
this for the other two orders.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:01.672
OK so this is all that
we just talked about

00:39:01.672 --> 00:39:04.208
and the best way to feel
your oneness with this

00:39:04.208 --> 00:39:08.779
is to do some practice problems
more than what we've just done.

00:39:08.779 --> 00:39:12.450
The next-- really?

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:16.454
The next topic, I'm not going
to spend the same amount of time

00:39:16.454 --> 00:39:18.689
on temperature and
catalysts obviously,

00:39:18.689 --> 00:39:22.960
but I want to mention how
these impacts reaction rates.

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:28.099
We're talking about how fast
the concentrations change.

00:39:28.099 --> 00:39:30.267
And to do that, what
we're going to do

00:39:30.267 --> 00:39:34.438
is we're going to use the
collision theory of reactions.

00:39:34.438 --> 00:39:37.208
And I've just written
it down here what it is.

00:39:37.208 --> 00:39:39.043
What is the collision
theory of reactions?

00:39:39.043 --> 00:39:42.279
What it is, is it says that they
occur when particles collide.

00:39:42.279 --> 00:39:47.118
Why does a plus b go to c?

00:39:47.118 --> 00:39:49.553
Well, because things collided
into each other and they

00:39:49.553 --> 00:39:53.491
got close enough or they maybe
had the right orientation

00:39:53.491 --> 00:39:57.661
so that new bonds could
form or others could break.

00:39:57.661 --> 00:40:00.164
And the theory also says that
not all collisions result

00:40:00.164 --> 00:40:01.899
in the formation of
product and that there

00:40:01.899 --> 00:40:05.002
are two factors that matter,
the energy of the collision,

00:40:05.002 --> 00:40:06.737
and the orientation
of the particles.

00:40:06.737 --> 00:40:08.439
I want to highlight
those two factors.

00:40:08.439 --> 00:40:09.673
So energy of the collision.

00:40:09.673 --> 00:40:13.577
We're going to start with
the energy of the collision

00:40:13.577 --> 00:40:15.379
and we're going to talk
about that in terms

00:40:15.379 --> 00:40:16.580
of where you get that energy.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:18.215
You get it from heat.

00:40:18.215 --> 00:40:20.851
You get it from
thermal energy, which

00:40:20.851 --> 00:40:24.155
means these things are
vibrating and moving,

00:40:24.155 --> 00:40:26.090
maybe if it's a gas,
they're bouncing off

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:30.361
the walls of the container,
that's temperature.

00:40:30.361 --> 00:40:31.829
How does that matter?

00:40:31.829 --> 00:40:33.731
Well, in order to
understand how that matters,

00:40:33.731 --> 00:40:39.370
we have to understand, we
have to think about this--

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:41.338
and this is what collision
theory for reactions

00:40:41.338 --> 00:40:45.509
tells us to do is to think
about a reaction in terms

00:40:45.509 --> 00:40:49.647
of an energy landscape
and an energy barrier

00:40:49.647 --> 00:40:52.950
that you have to overcome.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:58.689
So here I have this
Ea, which is the energy

00:40:58.689 --> 00:40:59.924
that it's going to take.

00:40:59.924 --> 00:41:05.062
I need to put that energy
in for the collision

00:41:05.062 --> 00:41:05.996
to be strong enough.

00:41:05.996 --> 00:41:08.466
Think about it as like, well
I just kind of didn't collide,

00:41:08.466 --> 00:41:12.503
I had a very low
energy, so I'm below Ea

00:41:12.503 --> 00:41:15.306
and I can't get over this hill.

00:41:15.306 --> 00:41:17.875
It really is if you think
about it like pushing a car,

00:41:17.875 --> 00:41:21.712
did I push it hard enough
so it can go over the hill,

00:41:21.712 --> 00:41:24.448
or is it just pushed
a little bit, that's

00:41:24.448 --> 00:41:27.718
the kinetic energy
we're talking about,

00:41:27.718 --> 00:41:28.986
could it get over that hill.

00:41:28.986 --> 00:41:32.423
Because what we're seeing
is that for this reaction

00:41:32.423 --> 00:41:34.859
to happen there's some amount
of energy that's needed,

00:41:34.859 --> 00:41:38.896
that's the activation energy,
that's the activation energy.

00:41:38.896 --> 00:41:40.297
OK.

00:41:40.297 --> 00:41:41.899
So what does that mean?

00:41:41.899 --> 00:41:44.101
Well, first of
all, you should all

00:41:44.101 --> 00:41:45.636
be feeling something right now.

00:41:48.305 --> 00:41:51.675
And you know what I'm
talking about, I'm talking

00:41:51.675 --> 00:41:54.879
about Svante, Svante Arrhenius.

00:41:54.879 --> 00:41:56.113
Right, somebody said it.

00:41:56.113 --> 00:41:57.548
You were feeling it.

00:41:57.548 --> 00:42:02.152
Why, because I'm talking about a
process that has a barrier that

00:42:02.152 --> 00:42:06.423
is thermally activated, a
process that has a barrier that

00:42:06.423 --> 00:42:07.091
is thermally--

00:42:07.091 --> 00:42:12.062
Svante Arrhenius, crickets.

00:42:12.062 --> 00:42:17.434
Crickets and then
intrinsic carriers.

00:42:17.434 --> 00:42:22.907
Activated processes, reactions,
right, they have a barrier.

00:42:22.907 --> 00:42:24.475
They have barrier.

00:42:24.475 --> 00:42:27.912
Now here's the thing
though, Ea is typically

00:42:27.912 --> 00:42:36.253
like maybe an electron volt, and
we know that KbT is 0.025 eV.

00:42:36.253 --> 00:42:39.723
We've talked about this before
and how is that possible.

00:42:39.723 --> 00:42:45.362
It's the same as what we talked
about before because this

00:42:45.362 --> 00:42:46.497
is an average energy.

00:42:46.497 --> 00:42:48.666
If you put the Boltzmann
constant in to room

00:42:48.666 --> 00:42:54.038
temperature, This is
at room temperature--

00:42:54.038 --> 00:42:57.775
If you put the Boltzmann
constant in at room temperature

00:42:57.775 --> 00:43:00.411
you're going to get
that small energy,

00:43:00.411 --> 00:43:03.380
but we know that thermal
energy is a distribution.

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:06.984
This is something we've talked
about, so here it is again.

00:43:06.984 --> 00:43:08.619
So this is the
Boltzmann distribution

00:43:08.619 --> 00:43:10.955
is what this
distribution is called.

00:43:10.955 --> 00:43:13.490
And if I have my
energy for activating

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:17.361
a reaction, my barrier for
the reaction to happen,

00:43:17.361 --> 00:43:18.929
shown there with
the vertical line,

00:43:18.929 --> 00:43:22.032
it means that I have
two temperatures.

00:43:22.032 --> 00:43:25.836
So yeah, kt may be small,
but there's some tails

00:43:25.836 --> 00:43:29.406
out there where there is
enough thermal energy,

00:43:29.406 --> 00:43:31.942
there's enough kinetic
energy for this collision

00:43:31.942 --> 00:43:32.943
to lead to the reaction.

00:43:36.013 --> 00:43:39.683
So those tails grow
larger and larger,

00:43:39.683 --> 00:43:42.786
the amount of molecules
that have enough energy

00:43:42.786 --> 00:43:45.389
grows larger as you
increase the temperature.

00:43:45.389 --> 00:43:48.025
That is Arrhenius.

00:43:48.025 --> 00:43:52.596
The probability for this
to happen grows, that is

00:43:52.596 --> 00:43:54.331
what Arrhenius gives us.

00:43:54.331 --> 00:43:55.366
And so here it is mapped.

00:43:55.366 --> 00:43:59.903
This is something that
is in the textbook that

00:43:59.903 --> 00:44:02.306
is a nice diagram because
it maps the two together.

00:44:02.306 --> 00:44:04.341
You've got the reactant
going to the products,

00:44:04.341 --> 00:44:07.478
there's an activated complex,
it needs that activation energy

00:44:07.478 --> 00:44:11.315
and over there we've turned on
the side these kinetic energy

00:44:11.315 --> 00:44:13.183
distribution plots.

00:44:13.183 --> 00:44:14.952
So you can see, when
do I have enough?

00:44:14.952 --> 00:44:16.720
Well, when I'm over
that activation energy

00:44:16.720 --> 00:44:19.556
and there is going to be
some fraction of molecules

00:44:19.556 --> 00:44:23.560
that have enough and that
fraction depends on temperature

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:24.628
and that is Arrhenius.

00:44:24.628 --> 00:44:29.099
And so what we get is
that the rate, the rate k,

00:44:29.099 --> 00:44:35.806
k the rate is equal
to some constant A

00:44:35.806 --> 00:44:41.178
times e to the
minus Ea over KbT.

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:47.818
And remember, we use
Kb for one collision,

00:44:47.818 --> 00:44:51.922
for a molecule or an atom.

00:44:51.922 --> 00:44:59.563
We would use R or r for a mole.

00:44:59.563 --> 00:45:04.435
Here I'm talking about a single
event, one molecule colliding

00:45:04.435 --> 00:45:07.538
with another to make
the reaction happen.

00:45:07.538 --> 00:45:10.874
Now, this is called
the frequency factor

00:45:10.874 --> 00:45:12.776
and it's a constant.

00:45:12.776 --> 00:45:17.147
Constant, constant,
and that's something

00:45:17.147 --> 00:45:22.453
that you could understand
how this might depend

00:45:22.453 --> 00:45:25.889
on a combination of things.

00:45:25.889 --> 00:45:29.760
But it's assumed,
incorrectly, but it's

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.762
a good enough
approximation for us,

00:45:31.762 --> 00:45:33.897
but it's assumed to be
independent of temperature

00:45:33.897 --> 00:45:36.266
so we pull it out and call
it a frequency factor.

00:45:36.266 --> 00:45:41.605
And it has in it information
about whether these things

00:45:41.605 --> 00:45:44.475
really made the right
collision or not, that's

00:45:44.475 --> 00:45:45.743
what A has in it.

00:45:45.743 --> 00:45:49.379
This exponent,
Arrhenius is telling us

00:45:49.379 --> 00:45:53.717
this exponent tells me about
that fraction, that fraction,

00:45:53.717 --> 00:45:55.352
but A tells me
about orientation.

00:45:55.352 --> 00:45:57.121
So here's an example.

00:45:57.121 --> 00:46:02.159
Here's N0 plus 03, and on the
top line you can see the N0--

00:46:02.159 --> 00:46:04.962
right so, the O's are
red and N is blue--

00:46:04.962 --> 00:46:07.498
and the N0 is coming in, but
it's coming in either way

00:46:07.498 --> 00:46:09.399
whether it's N or
whether it's 0,

00:46:09.399 --> 00:46:12.136
it's coming into
the wrong oxygen.

00:46:12.136 --> 00:46:14.171
It's hitting that
wrong oxygen and so

00:46:14.171 --> 00:46:16.340
it doesn't do anything
because the one in the middle

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:19.643
isn't going to be
very reactive, not

00:46:19.643 --> 00:46:20.878
at least at this temperature.

00:46:20.878 --> 00:46:23.547
But then you've got
a different case

00:46:23.547 --> 00:46:25.549
where it comes in a
little differently,

00:46:25.549 --> 00:46:28.051
and in fact, the
nitrogen is what's

00:46:28.051 --> 00:46:31.655
coming in and leading the
way to that oxygen on the end

00:46:31.655 --> 00:46:34.491
and that leads to the reaction.

00:46:34.491 --> 00:46:38.195
So you can imagine now it's not
just how fast they're moving,

00:46:38.195 --> 00:46:45.068
which is what the exponent
tells us, it's more complicated.

00:46:45.068 --> 00:46:45.836
Right?

00:46:45.836 --> 00:46:47.471
And this is called
the frequency factor

00:46:47.471 --> 00:46:50.440
because it's all these
complicated effects rolled

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:54.111
into the frequency of the
collision being right,

00:46:54.111 --> 00:46:57.915
not just the temperature.

00:46:57.915 --> 00:46:59.016
OK.

00:46:59.016 --> 00:47:02.052
And so this gives us the
temperature dependence

00:47:02.052 --> 00:47:04.755
of the rate constant,
this gives us

00:47:04.755 --> 00:47:06.957
the temperature and dependence
of the rate constant,

00:47:06.957 --> 00:47:08.625
which is very important.

00:47:08.625 --> 00:47:11.829
This is not time,
this is temperature.

00:47:11.829 --> 00:47:12.329
All right?

00:47:12.329 --> 00:47:14.264
This is a different thing
than everything else

00:47:14.264 --> 00:47:17.167
we talked about here, which
involved time dependence,

00:47:17.167 --> 00:47:21.205
that's temperature dependent.

00:47:21.205 --> 00:47:24.474
And finally, the last thing
and just to show you this

00:47:24.474 --> 00:47:29.079
is my very last point,
is that just like before,

00:47:29.079 --> 00:47:34.318
if I wanted to run a reaction
and make it go faster, one way,

00:47:34.318 --> 00:47:37.321
just like with semiconductors
is to increase the temperature,

00:47:37.321 --> 00:47:40.624
but I don't want to run
my phone at 600 Kelvin.

00:47:40.624 --> 00:47:43.927
So what do we do,
we use chemistry.

00:47:43.927 --> 00:47:47.164
I may have reactions that
I want to run a lot faster.

00:47:47.164 --> 00:47:50.601
In fact, I very often
do, and what do I do,

00:47:50.601 --> 00:47:54.438
I use chemistry and those
are called catalysts.

00:47:54.438 --> 00:47:57.007
What a catalyst does,
and this picture shows it

00:47:57.007 --> 00:47:59.710
in the context of
collision theory,

00:47:59.710 --> 00:48:01.979
I've got these barriers
I'm trying to get over,

00:48:01.979 --> 00:48:03.513
here it's flipped,
it doesn't matter.

00:48:03.513 --> 00:48:05.415
There's a barrier
here, so the reactant

00:48:05.415 --> 00:48:06.917
is higher than the
product, it's OK,

00:48:06.917 --> 00:48:10.587
there's still a barrier
that I have to overcome.

00:48:10.587 --> 00:48:14.558
What a catalyst does is
without being consumed,

00:48:14.558 --> 00:48:17.961
what a catalyst does is it's
another material in there where

00:48:17.961 --> 00:48:22.165
it allows that reaction to
happen with a lower barrier.

00:48:22.165 --> 00:48:24.368
That's what a catalyst
is and you better

00:48:24.368 --> 00:48:25.636
believe this is important.

00:48:25.636 --> 00:48:28.138
So a catalyst lowers
that without changing

00:48:28.138 --> 00:48:30.274
the temperature.

00:48:30.274 --> 00:48:33.577
And in just 20 seconds,
here's my why this matters.

00:48:33.577 --> 00:48:36.847
Catalytic converters is one of
the most important technologies

00:48:36.847 --> 00:48:40.417
for pollution that has been
invented in over the last 50

00:48:40.417 --> 00:48:41.351
years.

00:48:41.351 --> 00:48:45.188
It's changed the game in terms
of what comes out of that car.

00:48:45.188 --> 00:48:46.323
Why does it work?

00:48:46.323 --> 00:48:47.958
It wouldn't work,
you wouldn't get rid

00:48:47.958 --> 00:48:50.727
of these things,
these toxic things,

00:48:50.727 --> 00:48:52.663
you wouldn't get rid of
them unless you ran it

00:48:52.663 --> 00:48:55.832
at 1,000 degrees and you're not
doing that underneath your car

00:48:55.832 --> 00:48:58.068
next to the gas tank.

00:48:58.068 --> 00:49:00.470
But if you put the
right catalyst in,

00:49:00.470 --> 00:49:03.607
all of those reactions to get
rid of those toxic chemicals

00:49:03.607 --> 00:49:06.176
can happen much more
quickly and efficiently.

00:49:06.176 --> 00:49:07.444
Right?

00:49:07.444 --> 00:49:08.178
OK.

00:49:08.178 --> 00:49:09.546
Have a great weekend.

00:49:09.546 --> 00:49:10.414
See you guys on Monday.

